'''David Wilson Jolly''' (born October 31, 1972) is an American lawyer and politician who is the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for the [[2026 Florida gubernatorial election]]. Jolly served as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for [[Florida's 13th congressional district]] from 2014 to 2017 as a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]]. After leaving office, he became a prominent critic of President [[Donald Trump]] and a [[political commentator]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Former Rep. David Jolly soaks up exposure as Republican critical of Trump |url=https://www.tampabay.com/former-rep-david-jolly-soaks-up-exposure-as-critical-trump-republican/2313117/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170321170345/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/former-rep-david-jolly-soaks-up-exposure-as-critical-trump-republican/2313117 |archive-date=March 21, 2017 |access-date=March 21, 2017 |website=[[Tampa Bay Times]] |language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=David Jolly tougher on Donald Trump than Charlie Crist |url=https://www.tampabay.com/david-jolly-tougher-on-donald-trump-than-charlie-crist/2311599/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170322014035/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/david-jolly-tougher-on-donald-trump-than-charlie-crist/2311599 |archive-date=March 22, 2017 |access-date=March 21, 2017 |website=[[Tampa Bay Times]] |language=en-us}}</ref> Jolly was a lobbyist at Van Scoyoc Associates.<ref>https://www.tallahassee.com/picture-gallery/news/state/2026/10/05/florida-governor-candidates-byron-donalds-david-jolly-property-tax-insurance-data-centers/92007439007/</ref>

In September 2018, Jolly left the Republican Party.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ex-GOP Rep. David Jolly Tells Bill Maher He's Left The Republican Party |last=Hipes |first=Patrick |date=October 5, 2018 |url=https://deadline.com/2018/10/david-jolly-leaves-republican-party-bill-maher-msnbc-video-1202477694/ |access-date=October 6, 2018 |archive-date=October 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181006235511/https://deadline.com/2018/10/david-jolly-leaves-republican-party-bill-maher-msnbc-video-1202477694/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In April 2025, Jolly joined the Democratic Party to run in the [[2026 Florida gubernatorial election#Democratic primary|Democratic primary]] for the 2026 Florida gubernatorial election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Leonard |first=Kimberly |date=June 5, 2025 |title=Well-known Trump critic David Jolly enters race for Florida governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/06/05/david-jolly-enters-race-florida-governor-00386873 |access-date=June 5, 2025 |website=[[Politico]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Jolly was born in [[Dunedin, Florida]], the son of Judith and Lawson Jolly, a Baptist pastor.<ref name="TBrunning">{{cite news |date=November 2, 2013 |title=Rick Baker won't run for Young's seat, but David Jolly will |url=https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/local/rick-baker-wont-run-for-youngs-seat-but-david-jolly-will/2150510/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131104082023/http://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/local/rick-baker-wont-run-for-youngs-seat-but-david-jolly-will/2150510 |archive-date=November 4, 2013 |access-date=November 2, 2013 |newspaper=[[Tampa Bay Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | last = Bradshaw | first = Kate
| date = February 23, 2014 | title = Jolly's passion for politics started early | url = https://www.tbo.com/pinellas-county/jollys-passion-for-politics-started-early-20140223/ | url-status = dead | work = [[The Tampa Tribune]] | archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20181013211613/https://www.tbo.com/pinellas-county/jollys-passion-for-politics-started-early-20140223/ | archive-date = October 13, 2018 | access-date  = May 23, 2020
}}</ref> He grew up in [[Dade City, Florida]]. At age 16, Jolly was involved in an automobile accident that killed a pedestrian; authorities determined that he was not at fault.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lush |first=Tamara |title=Fla. candidate Jolly speaks about crash that killed man |url=https://www.tallahassee.com/story/local/2014/02/26/fla-candidate-jolly-speaks-about-crash-that-killed-man/5827739/ |access-date=February 9, 2024 |website=Tallahassee Democrat |language=en-US}}</ref>

He received his B.A. degree from [[Emory University]] in 1994 and his [[Juris Doctor|J.D.]] degree from the [[George Mason University School of Law]] in 2001.<ref>US Congress [http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=J000296 Jolly, David W., (1972 – )] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160815073413/http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=J000296 |date=August 15, 2016 }} Page accessed April 6, 2016</ref>

== U.S. House of Representatives (2014–2017)==
Before running for political office, Jolly worked for Republican U.S. Representative [[Bill Young (Florida politician)|Bill Young]] full time from 1995 to 2006 in various positions, with a brief break for six months in 2001 when he worked at a Washington securities firm. In 2002, Jolly became Young's general counsel and held that position until he left in 2006.<ref name=LobbyistFacts/> He served as the personal attorney for Young's family as well.<ref name=connected>Adam C. Smith for the Tampa Bay Times. January 3, 2014 [https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/elections/pinellas-congressional-candidate-david-jolly-connected-and-up-to-speed-but/2159534/ Pinellas congressional candidate David Jolly: Connected and up to speed, but local enough?], tampabay.com; accessed September 16, 2016.</ref>

In 2007, Jolly joined Washington, D.C. firm Van Scoyoc Associates as a [[lobbying in the United States|lobbyist]]. In 2011, he left Van Scoyoc to open his own lobbying firm, Three Bridges Advisors. During his time as a lobbyist he made political donations to both Republicans (about $36,000) and Democrats (about $30,000).<ref name=LobbyistFacts>{{cite web|title=David Jolly gave 'almost $30,000 to keep Democrats in Congress' as a lobbyist, Kathleen Peters says|url=http://www.politifact.com/florida/statements/2013/dec/27/kathleen-peters/david-jolly-gave-almost-30000-keep-democrats-congr|work=PolitiFact|date=December 27, 2013|publisher=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=April 3, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160403125944/http://www.politifact.com/florida/statements/2013/dec/27/kathleen-peters/david-jolly-gave-almost-30000-keep-democrats-congr/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="autogenerated1">{{cite news|last=Tau|first=Byron|title=GOP candidate's Democratic giving past|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2013/11/david-jolly-florida-donations-99922.html|newspaper=Politico|date=November 15, 2013}}</ref> Jolly had his name removed from the [[Lobby register#United States|lobby registry]] to run for the vacant House seat.<ref name=LobbyistFacts/> At the time, Jolly was working as vice president of the Clearwater-based investment company Boston Finance Group.<ref name=connected/>

=== 2014 special election ===
{{see also|2014 Florida's 13th congressional district special election}}

After being behind in the early tallies, Jolly won the 2014 election on March 11, with 48.4% of the vote. He was sworn into office on March 13.<ref name=swornin>{{cite news|last=Cassata|first=Donna|title=Florida's Jolly Sworn in as Newest Congressman|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/floridas-jolly-sworn-newest-congressman-22900163|access-date=January 22, 2015|newspaper=Associated Press|date=March 13, 2014|archive-date=March 26, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140326144221/http://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/floridas-jolly-sworn-newest-congressman-22900163|url-status=live}}</ref>

Jolly had won the Republican nomination on January 14, 2014, over [[Mark Bircher]] and [[Kathleen Peters]], winning a plurality of 45% of the vote.<ref name=AP/> Jolly faced Democratic nominee [[Alex Sink]] and a Libertarian candidate, Lucas Overby, in the special election. The race received national attention as possibly forecasting the mid-term elections that were coming in November of that year and became the most expensive Congressional race in history, with approximately $11M spent, $9M of it by outside groups.<ref name="TBOwinst1st">{{cite news |last=Bradshaw |first=Kate |date=March 11, 2014 |title=David Jolly takes District 13 election |url=http://www.tbo.com/pinellas-county/polls-close-in-contentious-congressional-race-20140311/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180923113137/https://www.tbo.com/pinellas-county/polls-close-in-contentious-congressional-race-20140311/ |archive-date=September 23, 2018 |access-date=May 23, 2020 |newspaper=St. Petersburg Tribune}}</ref> During the campaign there was friction between the [[National Republican Congressional Committee]] and Jolly; the RNC thought Jolly's campaign was inept and Jolly criticized the negative ads run by the RNC.<ref name="Isenstadt">{{cite web|last=Isenstadt|first=Alex|title=National GOP turns on Florida candidate|publisher=Politico.com|date=March 7, 2014|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2014/03/david-jolly-alex-sink-florida-special-election-2014-104397.html#ixzz2vRbfkXot|access-date=March 10, 2014|quote=Over the past week, a half-dozen Washington Republicans have described Jolly’s campaign against Democrat Alex Sink as a Keystone Cops operation, marked by inept fundraising, top advisers stationed hundreds of miles away from the district in the state capital and the poor optics of a just-divorced, 41-year-old candidate accompanied on the campaign trail by a girlfriend 14 years his junior. The sources would speak only on condition of anonymity.|archive-date=March 9, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140309074441/http://www.politico.com/story/2014/03/david-jolly-alex-sink-florida-special-election-2014-104397.html#ixzz2vRbfkXot|url-status=live}}</ref> and voters were generally unhappy with the overwhelming number of attack ads on both sides.<ref name=TBOwinst1st/>

=== 2014 general election ===
{{see also|2014 United States House of Representatives elections in Florida#District 13}}

Jolly ran for reelection to a full term in November 2014. He was unopposed in the Republican primary and no Democrat ran against him in the general election. His only challenger was Lucas Overby, the [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] nominee who came in third in the special election one year prior. Jolly defeated Overby with 75% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|title=U.S. House Election Results|url=http://elections.huffingtonpost.com/2014/results/house|access-date=February 29, 2016|work=[[The Huffington Post]]|archive-date=March 4, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304113733/http://elections.huffingtonpost.com/2014/results/house|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2016 U.S. Senate election ===
{{main|2016 United States Senate election in Florida}}

On July 20, 2015, Jolly announced that he was giving up his seat to run for the [[United States Senate]] seat being vacated by [[Marco Rubio]], who was not running for reelection due to [[Marco Rubio presidential campaign, 2016|his bid]] for the [[President of the United States|U.S. presidency]].<ref name=SenateRun>{{cite web |agency=Associated Press |url=http://jacksonville.com/breaking-news/2015-07-20/story/florida-congressman-david-jolly-announces-run-marco-rubios-us-senate |title=Florida Congressman David Jolly running for Senate; ex-Gov. Charlie Crist eyes his seat |work=[[The Florida Times-Union]] |date=July 20, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2016 |archive-date=April 24, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160424183353/http://jacksonville.com/breaking-news/2015-07-20/story/florida-congressman-david-jolly-announces-run-marco-rubios-us-senate |url-status=live }}</ref> As of August 2015, it appeared that Jolly would face several opponents in the August 30, 2016, Republican primary election, including U.S. Representative [[Ron DeSantis]] and Lieutenant Governor of Florida [[Carlos López-Cantera]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=Shastry|first1=Anjali|title=Race for Marco Rubio's Florida Senate seat shaping up as expensive, explosive|url=http://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2015/aug/17/2016-florida-senate-race-for-marco-rubios-seat-sha/?page=all|access-date=February 29, 2016|work=[[The Washington Times]]|date=August 17, 2015|archive-date=February 25, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160225222430/http://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2015/aug/17/2016-florida-senate-race-for-marco-rubios-seat-sha/?page=all|url-status=live}}</ref> However, on June 17, 2016, after Rubio reversed his decision, Jolly withdrew from the Senate race to run for re-election to the House, citing "unfinished business".<ref name="autogenerated2">{{cite web|url=http://www.cnn.com/2016/06/17/politics/david-jolly-marco-rubio-senate-race/|title=David Jolly drops out of Florida Senate race, possibly clearing way for Marco Rubio|work=[[CNN]]|last=Kopan|first=Tal|date=June 17, 2016|access-date=June 17, 2016|archive-date=June 17, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160617191047/http://www.cnn.com/2016/06/17/politics/david-jolly-marco-rubio-senate-race/|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2016 general election ===
{{see also|2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Florida#District 13}}

In his bid for a second full term, Jolly faced former Florida Governor and St. Petersburg resident [[Charlie Crist]], a former Republican who had turned Democratic after a brief stint as an independent. He found himself in a district that had been made significantly more Democratic after a court threw out Florida's original congressional map. Notably, the new district absorbed a heavily Democratic portion of southern Pinellas County, including almost all of St. Petersburg;<ref>{{cite news|url=http://wusfnews.wusf.usf.edu/post/crist-revives-political-career-win-over-jolly|title=Crist Revives Political Career With Win over Jolly|publisher=[[WUSF-FM]]|author1=Steve Newborn|author2=Mark Schreiner|date=November 9, 2016|access-date=November 30, 2016|archive-date=December 1, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201081600/http://wusfnews.wusf.usf.edu/post/crist-revives-political-career-win-over-jolly|url-status=live}}</ref> some of the more Democratic portions of St. Petersburg had previously been in the Tampa-based 14th District.{{Citation needed|date=April 2025}}

Ultimately, Jolly lost to Crist by 51.9% to 48.1%.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/florida-house-district-13-jolly-crist|title=Florida U.S. House 13th District Results: Charlie Crist Wins|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=November 11, 2016|access-date=November 11, 2016|archive-date=November 12, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161112092830/http://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/florida-house-district-13-jolly-crist|url-status=live}}</ref> Jolly's defeat ended a 62-year hold on this St. Petersburg-based district by the GOP. [[William C. Cramer|William Cramer]] had won the seat for the Republicans in 1954,<ref>State of Florida, General election returns, November 4, 1952, and November 2, 1954</ref> handing it to Young in 1970.<ref>{{cite news|title=U.S. Rep. C.W. Bill Young dies at 82|newspaper=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|date=October 18, 2013|access-date=January 5, 2014|url=https://www.tampabay.com/news/obituaries/congressional-legend-cw-bill-young-dies-at-82/2148018/|archive-date=January 8, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140108043819/http://www.tampabay.com/news/obituaries/congressional-legend-cw-bill-young-dies-at-82/2148018|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Tenure===
According to ''[[Politico]]'', Jolly was known for his [[Centrism|centrist]] stances in U.S. Congress,<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=April 24, 2025 |title=David Jolly registers as a Democrat, making moves toward Florida governor bid |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/24/david-jolly-registers-democrat-florida-governor-00306774 |access-date=April 24, 2025 |website=Politico |language=en |quote=Jolly served only one full term in Congress, where he became known for his centrist stances, but is a well-known figure in Florida and nationally because he’s been an MSNBC political contributor for years.}}</ref> where he served one partial and one full term as a Republican. During his Congressional tenure, Jolly opposed the [[Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act]], but later he stated on an interview with MSNBC that during unemployment he had a new appreciation for its use as a "safety net".<ref>{{cite news|last1=Weigel|first1=David|title=David Jolly and Obamacare, One More Time|url=http://www.slate.com/blogs/weigel/2014/03/13/david_jolly_and_obamacare_one_more_time.html|access-date=February 29, 2016|publisher=Slate|date=March 13, 2014|archive-date=March 7, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160307090713/http://www.slate.com/blogs/weigel/2014/03/13/david_jolly_and_obamacare_one_more_time.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Miller|first1=Hayley|title=This Former GOP Congressman Loathed Obamacare – Until He Lost His Own Coverage|url=http://m.huffpost.com/us/entry/us_59529e05e4b02734df2de1be|access-date=June 28, 2017|work=[[The Huffington Post]]|date=June 28, 2017|archive-date=November 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201106151103/https://www.huffpost.com/entry/david-jolly-obamacare_n_59529e05e4b02734df2de1be|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2016, Jolly said that he was "[[pro-life]]".<ref>{{cite news|last1=Smith|first1=Adam|title=David Jolly donated to Planned Parenthood executive|url=https://www.tampabay.com/david-jolly-donated-to-planned-parenthood-executive/2240731/|access-date=February 29, 2016|work=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|date=August 10, 2015|archive-date=March 5, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305131320/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/david-jolly-donated-to-planned-parenthood-executive/2240731|url-status=live}}</ref>

====Stop Act (2016)====
Jolly introduced the bipartisan "Stop Act" in 2016 to prohibit federally elected officials from directly soliciting campaign funds or donations on the belief that post-[[Citizens United v. FEC|Citizens United]] fundraising was taking an excessive amount of their time away from their elected duties.<ref>{{Cite web |last=O'Donnell |first=Norah |date=April 24, 2016 |title=Are members of Congress becoming telemarketers?  |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/60-minutes-are-members-of-congress-becoming-telemarketers/ |access-date=May 3, 2025 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

====Campaign Wikipedia edits====
In April 2016, Jolly's United States Senate campaign spokesperson Sarah Bascom confirmed that the campaign had made edits to his Wikipedia page to remove information about Jolly that included references to the [[Church of Scientology]] and to his lobbying activities, alleging that the posts presented a "public negative narrative" against him, and she accused an unnamed rival campaign of adding "[[propaganda]]" to the article.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tampabay.com/david-jolly-campaign-scrubs-wikipedia-mentions-of-scientology-lobbying/2272038/|title=David Jolly campaign scrubs Wikipedia mentions of Scientology, lobbying|website=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=April 6, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160712085650/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/david-jolly-campaign-scrubs-wikipedia-mentions-of-scientology-lobbying/2272038|archive-date=July 12, 2016|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.buzzfeed.com/andrewkaczynski/florida-senate-campaign-admits-to-scrubbing-candidates-wikip|title=Florida Senate Campaign Admits To Scrubbing Candidate's Wikipedia Page|website=BuzzFeed|date=April 5, 2016 |access-date=April 6, 2016|archive-date=April 6, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160406205222/http://www.buzzfeed.com/andrewkaczynski/florida-senate-campaign-admits-to-scrubbing-candidates-wikip|url-status=live}}</ref> Jolly called the removal "a careless staff mistake" and said that he stands by his record and wants the public to be fully informed.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Kaczynski|first1=Andrew|title=Florida Senate Candidate: Scrubbing Of Wikipedia Page Was A 'Careless Staff Mistake'|url=https://www.buzzfeed.com/andrewkaczynski/florida-senate-candidate-scrubbing-of-wikipedia-page-was-a-c#.eeVK4WLdd|publisher=BuzzFeed News|access-date=April 6, 2016|date=April 6, 2016|archive-date=April 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160408003531/http://www.buzzfeed.com/andrewkaczynski/florida-senate-candidate-scrubbing-of-wikipedia-page-was-a-c#.eeVK4WLdd|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Smith|first1=Adam C.|title=David Jolly: It was staff mistake to scrub my Wikipedia page|url=https://www.tampabay.com/david-jolly-it-was-staff-mistake-to-scrub-his-wikipedia-page/2272126/|access-date=April 6, 2016|newspaper=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|date=April 6, 2016|archive-date=April 10, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160410131234/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/david-jolly-it-was-staff-mistake-to-scrub-his-wikipedia-page/2272126|url-status=live}}</ref>
====Rankings====
In 2016, Jolly was ranked as the 48th most bipartisan member of the U.S. House of Representatives during the [[114th United States Congress]] (and the fourth most bipartisan House Rep. from Florida) by [[Richard Lugar#The Lugar Center|The Lugar Center]] and the [[McCourt School of Public Policy]].<ref>{{Citation|url=http://www.thelugarcenter.org/assets/htmldocuments/The%20Lugar%20Center%20-%20McCourt%20School%20Bipartisan%20Index%20114th%20Congress%20House%20Scores.pdf|title=The Lugar Center – McCourt School Bipartisan Index|publisher=[[Richard Lugar#The Lugar Center|The Lugar Center]]|date=March 7, 2016|access-date=April 30, 2017|archive-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412081713/http://www.thelugarcenter.org/assets/htmldocuments/The%20Lugar%20Center%20-%20McCourt%20School%20Bipartisan%20Index%20114th%20Congress%20House%20Scores.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

