== Between campaigns (2017–2026)==
[[File:David Jolly (32878962228).jpg|thumb|Jolly in 2018]]
Since leaving his Congressional office, Jolly has participated regularly as a political commentator on cable news sources such as [[CNN]] and [[MSNBC]];<ref name=":0" /> in this capacity he has been critical of President Donald Trump.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/239781-david-jolly-runs-2018-rank-file-republicans-support|title=If David Jolly runs again in 2018, would rank-and-file Republicans support him?|first=Mitch|last=Perry|date=June 9, 2017|access-date=May 22, 2020|archive-date=June 19, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190619173540/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/239781-david-jolly-runs-2018-rank-file-republicans-support|url-status=live}}</ref> He considered running for lieutenant governor of Florida in [[2018 Florida gubernatorial election|2018]] on a bipartisan ticket, with former Democratic congressman [[Patrick Murphy (Florida politician)|Patrick Murphy]] as the candidate for governor.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.rollcall.com/2018/04/24/former-florida-reps-murphy-and-jolly-floating-bipartisan-governor-bid/|title=Former Florida Reps. Murphy and Jolly Floating Bipartisan Governor Bid|website=Roll Call|date=April 24, 2018 |access-date=May 22, 2020|archive-date=November 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201106151019/https://www.rollcall.com/2018/04/24/former-florida-reps-murphy-and-jolly-floating-bipartisan-governor-bid/|url-status=live}}</ref> They conducted a speaking tour around the state.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=April 10, 2018 |title=David Jolly, Patrick Murphy bring bipartisanship roadshow to Jacksonville Tuesday |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/260792-david-jolly-patrick-murphy-bring-bipartisanship-roadshow-to-jacksonville-tuesday/ |access-date=February 9, 2024 |website=[[Florida Politics]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Jolly ultimately decided not to run for any public office in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/258916-politics-timing-david-jolly-bows-2018|title=Politics is about timing: David Jolly won't run for elected office in 2018|first=Phil|last=Ammann|date=March 14, 2018|access-date=May 22, 2020|archive-date=September 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200913090042/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/258916-politics-timing-david-jolly-bows-2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

In October 2018, Jolly appeared on [[HBO]]'s ''[[Real Time with Bill Maher]]'' and said that he and his wife, Laura, had quit the Republican party.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Taylor |first=Janelle Irwin |date=October 9, 2018 |title=David Jolly officially breaks up with the Republican Party |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/276927-david-jolly-dumps-the-gop/ |access-date=February 9, 2024 |website=[[Florida Politics]] |language=en-US}}</ref> In April 2025, Jolly registered as a [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leonard |first1=Kimberly |last2=Fineout |first2=Gary |title=David Jolly registers as a Democrat, making moves toward Florida governor bid |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/24/david-jolly-registers-democrat-florida-governor-00306774 |access-date=April 24, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=April 24, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

In July 2018, Jolly became executive vice president and principal of Shumaker Advisors Florida, LLC, a wholly owned subsidiary of the Shumaker, Loop, and Kendrick law firm, at their [[Tampa]] office. In making the announcement, the firm said he would be working on ballot initiatives and community issues, including the effort to build a new stadium for the [[Tampa Bay Rays]] major league baseball team.<ref>{{cite news |date=July 5, 2018 |title=David Jolly Headed to Shumaker Associates |url=http://sunshinestatenews.com/story/david-jolly-headed-shumaker-associates |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181113165831/http://sunshinestatenews.com/story/david-jolly-headed-shumaker-associates |archive-date=November 13, 2018 |access-date=November 12, 2018 |work=Sunshine State News}}</ref>

== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{main|2026 Florida gubernatorial election}}
After formally joining the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] that April, in June 2025 Jolly announced a campaign for [[governor of Florida]] for the [[2026 Florida gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Leonard |first=Kimberly |date=June 5, 2025 |title=Well-known Trump critic David Jolly enters race for Florida governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/06/05/david-jolly-enters-race-florida-governor-00386873 |access-date=June 5, 2025 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> His running mate is former Democratic congresswoman [[Gwen Graham]] from [[Florida's 2nd congressional district]], with whom he served in the House of Representatives, and the runner-up in the Democratic gubernatorial primary in [[2018 Florida gubernatorial election|2018]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 10, 2026 |title=Jolly picks Gwen Graham for running mate on Dem Florida governor ticket |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/06/10/florida-david-jolly-gwen-graham-governor-democrats-00956047 |access-date=June 11, 2026 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> Jolly refused to debate his Republican opponent Byron Donalds.<ref>https://www.wctv.tv/2026/09/21/david-jolly-declines-debates-with-byron-donalds-florida-governors-race/</ref>

==Political positions==
===Abortion===
Jolly states that he is [[pro-choice]], having shifted his position on the issue after leaving the Republican Party, and he supports Florida restoring [[reproductive freedom]] and codification of the ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' and ''[[Casey v. planned parenthood|Casey v. Planned Parenthood]]'' framework. He said that having grown up in an [[Evangelicalism in the United States|evangelical]] household as the son of a preacher, he had previously conflated being religious with opposing ''Roe.'' He affirmed his belief and change in perspective that abortion rights should be protected by law, adding that, if elected governor, he would introduce legislation to codify abortion rights. By July 2025, he was endorsed by the leaders of Florida's [[pro-choice]] movement, including [[Gwen Graham]], who wrote that they trusted him and his change in perspective.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=May 3, 2025 |title=David Jolly entices Florida Democrats with dream of winning 2026 governor's race |url=https://www.sun-sentinel.com/2025/05/03/david-jolly-entices-florida-democrats-with-dream-of-winning-2026-governors-race/ |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=Sun Sentinel |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>[https://www.miamiherald.com/opinion/op-ed/article310881700.html We’re pro-choice Floridians — and we trust David Jolly to defend our rights]</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Issues |url=https://davidjolly.com/issues/ |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=David Jolly for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== AI data centers ===
Jolly is [[Opposition to AI data centers|opposed to AI data centers]] in Florida.<ref name="News-1">{{Cite news |last=Heddles |first=Claire |date=September 17, 2026 |title=Fact Check: Where candidates Donalds and Jolly stand on data centers |url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article317279152.html |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[Miami Herald]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=September 10, 2026 |title=Byron Donalds doubles down on AI data centers at UCF Turning Point event |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2026/09/10/byron-donalds-doubles-down-on-ai-data-centers-at-ucf-turning-point-event/ |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[Orlando Sentinel]]}}</ref> In his 2026 campaign, he said, "I have pledged to issue a one-year [[Moratorium (law)|moratorium]] on [[hyperscale data center]]s on my first day in office."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fox |first=Greg |date=October 19, 2026 |title=Race for Florida governor: Data centers |url=https://www.wesh.com/article/race-for-florida-governor-data-centers/74184463 |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[WESH]]}}</ref> He said data centers may cause local issues, such as for energy and water resources, [[noise pollution]], and quality of life.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vazquez |first=Christina |date=September 22, 2026 |title=AI data centers become flashpoint in Florida governor race |url=https://www.local10.com/news/local/2026/09/22/ai-data-centers-become-flashpoint-in-florida-governor-race/ |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[WPLG|WPLG Local 10]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=September 17, 2026 |title=Florida governor candidates differ on AI, data centers and property taxes |url=https://www.fox35orlando.com/news/florida-governor-candidates-differ-ai-data-centers-property-taxes |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[Fox 35 Orlando]]}}</ref> He said he would commission studies into the topic, and that it "could lead to a full ban. The bottom line is I don't think we need data centers in the state of Florida."<ref name="News-1" /> Jolly said, "They are rushing to build these things for reasons of profit for the industry, but not out of [[public interest]]."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Saunders |first=Forrest |date=September 2, 2026 |title=Data centers divide Florida governor's race as voters question the AI boom |url=https://www.tampabay28.com/news/politics/data-centers-divide-florida-governors-race-as-voters-question-the-ai-boom |access-date=October 20, 2026 |work=[[Tampa Bay 28]]}}</ref>

=== Education ===
Jolly proposed a 10-year public education plan for Florida if elected governor, including increasing teacher pay by 30% to bring salaries closer to the national average.<ref name="Website-1" /> He said he would fund reinvestment into education with revenue from existing state taxes on tourists visiting Florida.<ref name="News-2">{{Cite news |last=Sheridan |first=Kerry |date=October 4, 2026 |title=Democrat David Jolly talks of 'public education renaissance' during Sarasota visit |url=https://www.wusf.org/education/2026-10-04/democrat-david-jolly-public-education-renaissance-sarasota-visit |access-date=October 21, 2026 |work=[[WUSF (FM)|WUSF]]}}</ref> He said, "I just don't think we have a crisis in convention centers. I think we have a crisis in public education, and so I do think we should have a statewide conversation about how we use tourist development tax dollars."<ref name="News-2" />

Jolly stated that he would direct Florida's state university system to prioritize the admittance of qualified in-state students.<ref name="News-3">{{Cite news |date=October 17, 2026 |title=Florida parents against out-of-state student surge find ally in David Jolly |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2026/10/17/florida-parents-against-out-of-state-student-surge-find-ally-in-david-jolly/ |access-date=October 21, 2026 |work=Orlando Sentinel}}</ref><ref name="Website-1" /> He said "we have got to lean into opening more slots for Florida students."<ref name="News-3" /> He has called for reforms to the universal [[school voucher]] program.<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Kairi |first=Lowery |title=Where do Donalds and Jolly stand on important issues in Florida’s governor race? |url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/article316857030.html |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=Miami Herald}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sexton |first=Christine |date=2025-09-18 |title=Democratic gubernatorial hopeful David Jolly defends school choice |url=https://floridaphoenix.com/2025/09/18/democratic-gubernatorial-hopeful-david-jolly-defends-school-choice/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=Florida Phoenix |language=en-US}}</ref> About the ability of state voucher-funded private schools to eject students who underperform academically, Jolly said, "You should not be allowed to just kick the kid out and say thank you for the $8,500."<ref name="Website-1" />

Jolly stated that he would not participate in [[Culture war|culture wars]], arguing that "to bring politicians into the classrooms and the bedrooms and the doctor's offices, where the results are often communities who have already been marginalized, and are now further marginalized, I just think that's the wrong direction."<ref>{{Cite web |title=David Jolly vows to stop “culture wars” as Florida governor |url=https://www.wfla.com/news/florida/david-jolly-vows-to-stop-culture-wars-as-florida-governor/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=WFLA}}</ref>

===Environment===
In 2015, Jolly pushed to extend the ban on oil drilling in [[Gulf of Mexico|the gulf off Florida]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://news.wgcu.org/post/us-reps-push-extend-ban-oil-drilling-eastern-gulf-mexico|title=U.S. Reps Push To Extend Ban On Oil Drilling In Eastern Gulf Of Mexico|last=Meszaros|first=Jessica|website=news.wgcu.org|date=July 9, 2015 |access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315105321/http://news.wgcu.org/post/us-reps-push-extend-ban-oil-drilling-eastern-gulf-mexico|url-status=live}}</ref> In January 2015, Jolly announced plans to introduce a bill that would extend the reforms of the federal [[flood insurance]] program.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tampabay.com/rep-david-jolly-seeks-more-flood-insurance-legislation/2212488/|title=Rep. David Jolly seeks more flood insurance legislation|website=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315100452/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/rep-david-jolly-seeks-more-flood-insurance-legislation/2212488|url-status=live}}</ref> Jolly encouraged the U.S. House to extend the Treasure Island [[beach nourishment|beach renourishment]] project.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://coastalnewstoday.com/florida-david-jolly-calls-on-u-s-house-to-reinstate-pinellas-county-beach-nourishment/|title=Florida – David Jolly calls on U.S. House to reinstate Pinellas County beach nourishment {{!}} Coastal Engineering News & Subscription List|website=coastalnewstoday.com|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315112810/http://coastalnewstoday.com/florida-david-jolly-calls-on-u-s-house-to-reinstate-pinellas-county-beach-nourishment/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Firearms===
Jolly initially supported a broad interpretation of the [[Second Amendment to the United States Constitution|Second Amendment]] but has since advocated for stricter firearm regulations. He has expressed support for measures such as licensing, registration, and insurance requirements for gun ownership. He has cited the [[2016 Pulse nightclub shooting]] as a turning point, noting his participation as the only Republican joining Democrats on the House floor to call for legislative action. Jolly now supports comprehensive background checks, including for private and intra-family transfers, as well as an [[Assault weapons ban (USA)|assault weapons ban]]. He has stated that he views unlimited and largely unrestricted access to firearms as a primary contributor to gun violence. He said that Republicans are lying when they say that gun owners, not a lack of gun control, are the problem in cases of gun violence and mass shootings.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" />Speaking about firearms policy, Jolly said: "I do believe the Second Amendment is a fundamental right, but I don't believe it's beyond the reach of regulation, and I believe it's appropriate to look at regulations that ultimately keep the guns out of the hands of criminals."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gillin |first1=Joshua |title=U.S. Rep. David Jolly talks about veterans issues, Iraq and Jeb Bush |url=https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/jolly-appears-at-tiger-bay-today-hosts-veterans-forum-tuesday/2184564/ |access-date=February 29, 2016 |work=[[Tampa Bay Times]] |date=June 16, 2014 |archive-date=March 5, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305132903/http://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/jolly-appears-at-tiger-bay-today-hosts-veterans-forum-tuesday/2184564 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Jolly said he wants the votes of gun owners because he respects their rights and believes that they are not the problem, but rather that Florida's lack of gun violence prevention laws are. He argues that no law-abiding gun-owning voters will be hurt by his proposals which will "protect their children".<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=June 16, 2025 |title=Democratic governor candidate says he'll seek support from Florida gun owners — and stricter gun regulations |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/democratic-governor-candidate-says-ll-110000353.html |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Foreign policy and border security===
Jolly supported sending special operations forces overseas, securing the border,{{which|date=May 2025}} increasing the vetting process for legal immigrants, and increasing surveillance of suspected domestic terrorists.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://floridapolitics.com/archives/196618-email-insights-david-jolly-rips-administration-on-national-security|title=Email insights: David Jolly rips administration on national security |website=Florida Politics|date=December 14, 2015 |language=en-US|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315104217/http://floridapolitics.com/archives/196618-email-insights-david-jolly-rips-administration-on-national-security|url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2014, Jolly introduced the "Naval Station Guantanamo Bay Protection Act" bill.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tampabay.com/jolly-looks-to-hinder-obama-on-gitmo/2255825/|title=Jolly looks to hinder Obama on Gitmo|website=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315093151/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/jolly-looks-to-hinder-obama-on-gitmo/2255825|url-status=live}}</ref>

===LGBTQ+ rights===
In July 2014, Jolly declared support for legalizing [[same-sex marriage]]. Although he does not personally support same sex-marriage due to his Christian faith, he explained that "the sanctity of one’s marriage should be defined by their faith and by their church, not by their state", and that this position was consistent with his support of [[limited government]] and the protection of personal liberties.<ref>{{cite news |last=Sullivan |first=Sean |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/07/21/republican-rep-david-jolly-fla-supports-gay-marriage |title=Republican Rep. David Jolly (Fla.) announces support for gay marriage |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=July 21, 2014 |access-date=October 28, 2015 |archive-date=March 10, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160310144005/https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/07/21/republican-rep-david-jolly-fla-supports-gay-marriage |url-status=live }}</ref>

Jolly is opposed to [[Transgender people in sports|transgender women in women's sports]] and states that he will not change the ''[[Fairness in Women's Sports Act]]'', but that he "[hopes] to restore decency in how we approach it."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ndonwie |first=Churchill |date=2026-09-25 |title=Fact Check: Where Donalds and Jolly stand on transgender rights |url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/article317376303.html |access-date=2026-10-08 |website=Miami Herald}}</ref> When asked about his support of [[Transgender health care|gender-affirming care]] for minors, Jolly states that he supported parents' rights to make medical decisions for their children without government interference, and that "I simply subscribe to the old-school Republican position that government should stay out of your life, out of your doctor's office, and out of your bedroom".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bollinger |first=Alex |title=GOP candidate to replace Ron DeSantis is making trans rights the main focus of his campaign |url=https://www.lgbtqnation.com/2026/08/gop-candidate-to-replace-ron-desantis-is-making-trans-rights-the-main-focus-of-his-campaign/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=LGBTQ Nation |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Byron Donalds attacks David Jolly’s support for trans youth |url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/election/byron-donalds-david-jolly-transgender |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=The Advocate |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sloan |first=Steven |date=2026-08-13 |title=Mission improbable: A onetime Republican is trying to prove Democrats can win in Florida |url=https://www.wlrn.org/government-politics/2026-08-13/mission-improbable-a-onetime-republican-is-trying-to-prove-democrats-can-win-in-florida |access-date=2026-08-28 |language=en |via=WLRN |agency=Associated Press}}</ref>  

In August 2026, following an attack ad by his Republican opponent [[Byron Donalds]] centered on his implied support of [[Legal status of transgender people|transgender rights]] (stemming from a quote taken out of context from his appearance on a LGBTQ+ podcast), Jolly stated that "local communities should be able to celebrate their own and that government should stay out of your medical decisions."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Putterman |first=Samantha |date=2026-08-24 |title=Byron Donalds’ ad deceptively edited David Jolly clip on gender-affirming care |url=https://www.wlrn.org/government-politics/2026-08-24/byron-donalds-ad-deceptively-edited-david-jolly-clip-on-gender-affirming-care |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=WLRN |language=en}}</ref>

===Party affiliation===
In 2025, Jolly changed his party affiliation to Democratic. In response to autopsies of the [[2024 U.S. presidential election]], he said, "Leading Democrats today who come out and say, 'oh, we've got to change who we are, we have to change our values,' they're wrong. They're wrong. The Democratic Party's values are absolutely right and don't need to be changed."<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 3, 2025 |title=David Jolly entices Florida Democrats with dream of winning 2026 governor's race |url=https://www.sun-sentinel.com/2025/05/03/david-jolly-entices-florida-democrats-with-dream-of-winning-2026-governors-race/ |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=Sun Sentinel |language=en-US}}</ref>

Jolly states that, unlike [[Charlie Crist]], who has commented, "I didn't leave the party. The [[Republican Party (United States)|[Republican] party]] left me," his experience differs as Jolly himself left the Republican Party after changing his views. He argues that this is a strength, not a weakness, as opposed to what his critics say, because America "needs more politicians willing to admit they were wrong" and evolve to meet the moment where voters currently are. He further contends that many voters have also changed their beliefs in the past few years.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 5, 2025 |title=Pinellas County's David Jolly sets his sights on the governor's mansion as a Democrat |url=https://www.wusf.org/politics-issues/2025-06-05/tampa-bays-david-jolly-to-run-for-florida-governor |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=WUSF |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=David Jolly runs for Florida governor as a Democrat {{!}} Miami Herald | url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article307803510.html | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250605113010/https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article307803510.html | access-date=January 1, 2026 | archive-date=June 5, 2025}}</ref>

===U.S. debt===
Jolly supported the [[Balanced Budget Amendment]]<ref>{{cite news|last1=Krueger|first1=Curtis|title=New Jolly ad hits Sink on balanced budget, federal spending|url=https://www.tampabay.com/new-jolly-ad-hits-sink-on-balanced-budget-federal-spending/2164625/|access-date=February 29, 2016|work=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|date=February 7, 2014|archive-date=March 5, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305125207/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/new-jolly-ad-hits-sink-on-balanced-budget-federal-spending/2164625|url-status=live}}</ref> and said he would have voted to raise the [[debt limit]] in early 2014.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/national/david-jolly-campaigns-as-a-cw-bill-young-republican-but-which-one/2168337/|title=David Jolly campaigns as a C.W. Bill Young Republican, but which one?|last1=Smith|first1=Adam C.|date=March 3, 2014|newspaper=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 9, 2014|archive-date=March 10, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140310103434/http://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/national/david-jolly-campaigns-as-a-cw-bill-young-republican-but-which-one/2168337|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Veterans===
In July 2015, Jolly introduced the "Veterans Health Care Freedom Act" bill.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/us-senate-candidate-david-jolly-introduces-veterans-health-care-legislation/2238592|title=U.S. Senate candidate David Jolly introduces veterans health care legislation|website=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 15, 2016|archive-date=March 15, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315101358/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/us-senate-candidate-david-jolly-introduces-veterans-health-care-legislation/2238592|url-status=dead}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Jolly and his first wife, Carrie, divorced on January 16, 2014.<ref>{{Cite web |title=CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE DAVID JOLLY'S GIRLFRIEND ONCE NAMED ONE OF WASHINGTON'S MOST BEAUTIFUL PEOPLE |url=https://www.tampabay.com/archive/2014/01/09/congressional-candidate-david-jolly-s-girlfriend-once-named-one-of-washington-s-most-beautiful-people/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=Tampa Bay Times |language=en}}</ref> The divorce stemmed from a rumored affair with his then work subordinate Laura Donahoe.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-06-04 |title=A Jolly summer wedding |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2015/06/david-jolly-laura-donahoe-wedding-118615 |access-date=2026-10-22 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref>

Jolly married Laura Donahoe on July 3, 2015.<ref name=TBmarriage>{{cite news|url=https://www.tampabay.com/update-to-profile-on-pinellas-congressional-candidate-david-jolly/2160390/|title=Congressional candidate David Jolly's girlfriend once named one of Washington's most beautiful people|last1=Krueger|first1=Curtis|date=January 9, 2014|work=[[Tampa Bay Times]]|access-date=March 9, 2014|archive-date=March 11, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140311043734/http://www.tampabay.com/blogs/the-buzz-florida-politics/update-to-profile-on-pinellas-congressional-candidate-david-jolly/2160390|url-status=live}}</ref> They have two children.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 9, 2024 |title=X bio |url=https://twitter.com/laurajollyfl}}</ref>

